Every quirk in this chapter follows from what you've already seen: a model predicts likely tokens,
reads tokens rather than letters, and only knows its training text. The lab below has recorded
GPT-2 runs for each one; run GPT-2 live to try your own.

<GlassBoxGenerator
	title="Quirks lab"
	presets={[
		{ trace: 'quirk-moon', label: 'Made up: the first person on the moon' },
		{ trace: 'quirk-painter', label: 'Made up: a painter who doesn’t exist' },
		{ trace: 'quirk-iphone', label: 'Out of date: the latest iPhone' },
		{ trace: 'quirk-math', label: 'Arithmetic: 2 + 2' },
		{ trace: 'quirk-france', label: 'Wording: “The capital of France is”' },
		{ trace: 'quirk-france-qa', label: 'Wording: as a question and answer' },
		{ trace: 'quirk-loop', label: 'Stuck in a loop' }
	]}
/>

## Likely isn't the same as true

Ask GPT-2 who first walked on the moon and it writes that it was "a man named John, who was born in
1829." Ask about a painter who doesn't exist, and it describes his famous painting. This is called <GlossaryPopover term="hallucination">hallucination</GlossaryPopover>.<Cite id="ji2023" /> The model
was trained to produce text that *looks like* its training text, and a confident sentence about a
painter looks like many real ones. Nothing in the loop from <ChapterRef to="generating-text" /> checks whether it's true.

Tuning helps: in the InstructGPT tests, the tuned models made up information about half as often as
GPT-3, 21% of the time instead of 41%.<Cite id="ouyang2022" /> But the cause is the same, so it never
goes away completely. That's one reason assistants are given tools to look things up.

## Frozen in time

GPT-2's training text only includes web links from before 2018.<Cite id="radford2019" /> Ask it
for the latest iPhone and its top guesses are the iPhone 6, 5, and 7, the ones that filled the pages
it read. Every model has a cutoff like this: it knows nothing about later events unless they're put
into its prompt.

## Tokens, not letters

Ask a model how many r's are in "strawberry" and it may get it wrong, because it never sees the
letters:

<SpellingBlindSpot />

Numbers have the same problem: they are split into tokens in uneven ways, so the model can't simply
line up the digits and carry, the way you would on paper. In the lab, after "2 + 2 =", GPT-2 rates
" 3" more likely than " 4".

## Wording matters

"The capital of France is" and "Q: What is the capital of France? A:" ask the same thing, but they
look like different kinds of text. In the lab, the first makes GPT-2 ramble about "the capital of
the French Republic"; the second gets "Paris" in its first sentence. Much larger models can also
swing with small formatting changes: one study found accuracy differences of up to 76 percentage
points from formatting alone.<Cite id="sclar2024" />

## Limited memory and loops

A model only sees its context window (<ChapterRef to="the-context-problem" />): for GPT-2, 1,024 tokens. Anything earlier is cut off
and simply gone. And as <ChapterRef to="generating-text" /> showed, always taking the most likely token can trap a model in a
loop, like the cat that sits on the bed forever.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

<RecapCard>

- Models produce likely text, not checked facts, so they can confidently make things up.
- They know nothing after their training cutoff unless told.
- They read tokens, not letters or digits, which makes spelling and arithmetic hard.
- Small changes in wording can change the answer, and anything outside the context window is
  forgotten.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** several of these quirks come from answering straight away. What is a model doing
when it pauses to "think" first?
